      <tei:div type="colophon" subtype="introduction">
        <tei:p>“One expounds (<tei:hi rend="italic">doresh</tei:hi>) from ‘A wandering Aramean was my father’<tei:anchor xml:id="note_1"/> until he finishes the whole story” <tei:bibl>(Mishnah Pesaḥim 10:4)</tei:bibl>. The verse Deuteronomy 26:9 completes that story, and together with some midrash it fulfills the obligation. Jews stopped saying it during our long exile, but now we must express our gratitude to God for gathering millions of our exiles in the State of Israel, where they and their children are exiles no more. The verse and its midrash fit into the Passover Haggadah after the ten plagues and the midrash on them, right before the song <tei:hi rend="italic">Dayyenu</tei:hi>.</tei:p>
      </tei:div>
      <j:endConditional target="#osp_30982_introduction"/>
